\par
\par\b\fs32 \u9889? Topic 1: Electrostatics Basics (Week 1) \b0\fs24\par\par
\par
\par\b\fs28  Key Concepts \b0\fs24\par\par
\bullet \b Electric Charge: \b0  Fundamental property of matter. Understand nature, quantization, and conservation. \par\par
\bullet \b Vector Superposition Principle: \b0  Essential for solving problems with 3 or more charges. To find net force, add individual force vectors. \par\par
\par
\par\b\fs28  High-Yield Formulas \b0\fs24\par\par
\bullet \b Coulomb\u8217?s Law: \b0  \f1 F = k * (|q1 * q2| / r\u178?) \f0  \par\par
    \bullet Coulomb's constant \f1 k = 8.99 x 10\u8313? N\u183?m\u178?/C\u178? \f0  \par\par
    \bullet Permittivity of free space \f1 \u949?\u8320? = 8.85 x 10\u8315?\u185?\u178? C\u178?/(N\u183?m\u178?) \f0  \par\par
    \bullet Relationship: \f1 k = 1 / (4\u960?\u949?\u8320?) \f0  \par\par
\bullet \b Quantization of Charge: \b0  \f1 q = n * e \f0  \par\par
    \bullet Elementary charge \f1 e = 1.602 x 10\u8315?\u185?\u8313? C \f0  \par\par
\par
\par\b\fs28  Exam Practice Focus \b0\fs24\par\par
1. Calculate the force between two point charges separated by a distance. \par\par
2. Find the net force on a charge at the center of a triangle/square of charges. \par\par
3. Calculate how many electrons are in a given amount of Coulomb charge. \par\par
\par
---\par
\i \b  Pro Tip: \b0  Force is a \b VECTOR \b0 . If charges are in 2D, you \b must \b0  resolve them into x and y components before adding. \i0 \par\par
\par
---\par
\par
\par\b\fs32  Topic 2: Fields, Potential & Dipoles (Week 2) \b0\fs24\par\par
\par
\par\b\fs28  Key Concepts \b0\fs24\par\par
\bullet \b Electric Fields: \b0  The space surrounding charges visualized by field lines. It represents force per unit charge. \par\par
\bullet \b Electric Potential vs Potential Energy: \b0  Potential is a scalar representation of the electric field's effect (energy per unit charge). \par\par
\bullet \b Electric Dipoles: \b0  A pair of equal and opposite charges. \par\par
\bullet \b Dielectrics: \b0  Insulators that can be polarized by an applied electric field, reducing the internal field. \par\par
\par
\par\b\fs28  High-Yield Formulas \b0\fs24\par\par
\bullet \b Electric Field (Point Charge): \b0  \f1 E = k * (|q| / r\u178?) \f0  (Vector) \par\par
\bullet \b Electric Potential: \b0  \f1 V = k * (q / r) \f0  (Scalar) \par\par
\bullet \b Field-Potential Relation: \b0  \f1 E = -\u8711?V = -(dV/dx i + dV/dy j + dV/dz k) \f0  \par\par
\bullet \b Dipole Moment: \b0  \f1 p = q * d \f0  (Vector pointing from -q to +q) \par\par
\bullet \b Dipole Potential: \b0  \f1 V = (k * p * cos\u952?) / r\u178? \f0  \par\par
\par
\par\b\fs28  Exam Practice Focus \b0\fs24\par\par
1. Find the electric field at a point between two opposite charges. \par\par
2. Calculate the potential at a specific distance from a dipole. \par\par
3. Explain how a dielectric material changes the electric field inside a capacitor. \par\par
\par
---\par
\i \b  Pro Tip: \b0  Potential is a \b SCALAR \b0 . You can just add the values together without worrying about angles! Remember to include the \b sign \b0  (+ or -) of the charge when calculating scalar potential. \i0 \par\par
\par
---\par
\par
\par\b\fs32  Topic 3: Gauss's Law (Week 3) \b0\fs24\par\par
\par
\par\b\fs28  Key Concepts \b0\fs24\par\par
\bullet \b Electric Flux (\u934?E): \b0  Measure of the electric field passing through a given surface. \f1 \u934?E = E * A * cos\u952? \f0  \par\par
\bullet \b Gaussian Surface: \b0  A closed imaginary surface in 3D space through which the flux is calculated. \par\par
\par
\par\b\fs28  High-Yield Formulas \b0\fs24\par\par
\bullet \b Gauss's Law: \b0  \f1 \u934?E = \u8750? E \u183? dA = Q_enc / \u949?\u8320? \f0  \par\par
\par
\par\b\fs28  The "Big 4" Derivations to Memorize \b0\fs24\par\par
*You MUST be able to derive E for these from scratch using Gauss's Law:*\par
1. \b Point Charge: \b0  \f1 E = kQ / r\u178? \f0  (Spherical surface) \par\par
2. \b Infinite Line Charge: \b0  \f1 E = \u955? / (2\u960?\u949?\u8320?r) \f0  (Cylindrical surface) \par\par
3. \b Infinite Sheet of Charge: \b0  \f1 E = \u963? / (2\u949?\u8320?) \f0  (Pillbox surface) \par\par
4. \b Uniformly Charged Sphere: \b0  \par\par
   * Outside (\f1 r \u8805? R \f0 ): \f1 E = kQ / r\u178? \f0 \par
   * Inside (\f1 r < R \f0 ): \f1 E = (kQ / R\u179?) * r \f0 \par
\par
\par\b\fs28  Exam Practice Focus \b0\fs24\par\par
1. Define Electric Flux and its units (\f1 N\u183?m\u178?/C \f0 ). \par\par
2. Derive the electric field for an infinite sheet of charge using a "pillbox" Gaussian surface. \par\par
3. Calculate the flux through a cube with a charge at its center. \par\par
\par
---\par
\i \b  Pro Tip: \b0  Choosing the right \b Gaussian Surface \b0  is the key. For lines, use a cylinder. For sheets, use a pillbox. For spheres, use a sphere. Always exploit symmetry! \i0 \par\par
\par
---\par
\par
\par\b\fs32  Topic 4: Intro to Networks (Week 4) \b0\fs24\par\par
\par
\par\b\fs28  Key Concepts \b0\fs24\par\par
\bullet \b Networks: \b0  Master the "Divider Rules" to save time during circuit calculations. \par\par
\bullet \b Series & Parallel Resistors: \b0  How to simplify complex networks into a single equivalent resistance. \par\par
\par
\par\b\fs28  High-Yield Formulas \b0\fs24\par\par
\bullet \b Ohm's Law: \b0  \f1 V = I * R \f0  \par\par
\bullet \b Equivalent Resistance: \b0  \par\par
    \bullet Series: \f1 Req = R1 + R2 + ... + Rn \f0  \par\par
    \bullet Parallel: \f1 1/Req = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + ... + 1/Rn \f0  (For two: \f1 Req = (R1*R2)/(R1+R2) \f0 ) \par\par
\bullet \b Voltage Divider Rule (VDR - Series): \b0  \f1 Vx = Vs * (Rx / Req) \f0  \par\par
\bullet \b Current Divider Rule (CDR - Two Parallel Resistors): \b0  \f1 I1 = Is * (R2 / (R1 + R2)) \f0  *(Note: The OTHER resistor is on top!)* \par\par
\bullet \b Power: \b0  \f1 P = V*I = I\u178?R = V\u178?/R \f0  \par\par
\par
\par\b\fs28  Exam Practice Focus \b0\fs24\par\par
1. Simplify a complex network of 5-6 resistors into one \f1 Req \f0 . \par\par
2. Find the voltage across a specific resistor using VDR. \par\par
3. Find the current through a parallel branch using CDR. \par\par
\par
---\par
\i \b  Pro Tip: \b0  In CDR, the current through one branch is proportional to the \b other \b0  branch's resistance. Don't mix them up! \i0 \par\par
\par
