\par\b\fs32 \u9881?\u-497? Topic 5: Advanced Circuit Analysis (Week 5) \b0\fs24\par\par
\par
\par\b\fs28  Key Concepts \b0\fs24\par\par
\bullet \b Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL - Loop Rule): \b0  Sum of voltage drops and rises in a closed loop equals zero (\f1 \u931?V = 0 \f0 ). Based on Conservation of Energy. \par\par
\bullet \b Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL - Junction Rule): \b0  Sum of currents entering a junction equals sum of currents leaving (\f1 \u931?I_in = \u931?I_out \f0 ). Based on Conservation of Charge. \par\par
\bullet \b Mesh Analysis: \b0  Solving circuits using loop currents and KVL. \par\par
\bullet \b Nodal Analysis: \b0  Solving circuits using node voltages and KCL. \par\par
\par
\par\b\fs28  Key Analytical Steps \b0\fs24\par\par
\b Mesh Analysis: \b0 \par
1. Assign loop currents (i1, i2...) in the same direction (e.g., clockwise). \par\par
2. Write KVL equations for each loop. \par\par
3. Solve the system of linear equations. \par\par
\par
\b Nodal Analysis: \b0 \par
1. Pick a Ground (0V) reference node. \par\par
2. Assign node voltages (V1, V2...) to the other junctions. \par\par
3. Write KCL equations at each non-reference node. \par\par
\par
\par\b\fs28  Exam Practice Focus \b0\fs24\par\par
1. Solve a 2-loop circuit using Mesh Analysis. \par\par
2. Find the node voltages in a circuit with 3 nodes. \par\par
3. Apply Kirchhoff's Laws to find an unknown current in a bridge circuit. \par\par
\par
---\par
\i \b  Pro Tip: \b0  If a circuit has many parallel branches, \b Nodal Analysis \b0  is usually faster. If it has many series loops, \b Mesh Analysis \b0  is better. Picking the right one saves you 10 minutes in the exam! \i0 \par\par

